{"title":"Breaking one factor into equal groups","key_idea":"You can split one factor into smaller factors and keep the multiplication equal by filling in the missing number.","method":["Look at the factor being broken into pieces on the right side.","Divide that original factor by the new smaller factor to find the missing number.","Check: multiply all the factors on the right to see if you get the left side."],"worked_example":{"problem":"4 x 15 = 4 x 5 x __","steps":["The factor 15 is being split into 5 on the right side.","Divide 15 by 5 to find the missing number: 15 ÷ 5 = 3.","Check: 4 x 5 x 3 = 20 x 3 = 60, and 4 x 15 = 60, so it is correct."],"answer":"3"},"key_terms":[{"term":"factor","meaning":"a number you multiply in a multiplication"},{"term":"split or break","meaning":"divide a number into smaller factors"}],"common_mistakes":["Forgetting to divide the original factor by the new one, and just guessing a number instead."],"remember":"The product (answer) stays the same when you split one factor into smaller factors.","quick_check":[{"q":"In 7 x 10 = 7 x 2 x __, what is the missing number?","a":"5 (because 10 ÷ 2 = 5)"},{"q":"In 9 x 20 = 9 x 5 x __, what is the missing number?","a":"4 (because 20 ÷ 5 = 4)"}],"tell_your_child":"When you see a missing number like this, always divide the big factor by the small one they show you. Then check your answer by multiplying all three numbers together to make sure you get the same answer as the left side."}
